[{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Single-self-interacting-unit","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"This example considers the simplest non-trivial configuration: a single unit interacting with itself, without plasticity.","title":"Single self-interacting unit"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Initialization","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"using Random\nusing Statistics\nusing Plots\nRandom.seed!(0)\n\nusing HawkesPlasticNetworks; global const H = HawkesPlasticNetworks","title":"Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Define-the-parameters","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"n_units = 1\nτ_kernel = 10.0\nw_self = 0.5\nh_in = 0.6\ninitial_rate = 0.0001\nn_spikes = 20_000\nbin_width = 2.0\n\nFor a stable linear Hawkes process with a normalized exponential kernel, the stationary rate is h_in / (1 - w_self).\n\ntheoretical_rate = h_in/(1-w_self)","title":"Define the parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Define-the-population-and-connection","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"population = H.PopulationExpKernelExcitatory(\n    n_units,τ_kernel; label=\"unit\")\nconnection = H.ConnectionWithWeights(\n    population,fill(w_self,n_units,n_units),population)\n\nA network contains connected populations, rather than bare populations. The argument order is always post-synaptic first, then pre-synaptic.\n\nconnected_population = H.ConnectedPopulationExpKernel(\n    population,fill(h_in,n_units),(connection,population))","title":"Define the population and connection"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Record-the-spike-train-and-mean-firing-rate","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"rec_fulltrain = H.RecorderPopulationTrain(\n    population.label,n_spikes)\n\nRate recorders allocate their bins in advance. Twice the expected duration leaves ample room for this seeded example.\n\nrate_recording_end = 2*n_spikes/theoretical_rate\nrec_binnedrate = H.RecorderPopulationRate(\n    population,rate_recording_end; Δt=bin_width)","title":"Record the spike train and mean firing rate"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Define-the-network","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"network = H.RecurrentNetworkExpKernel(\n    (connected_population,),(rec_fulltrain,rec_binnedrate))","title":"Define the network"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Run-the-simulation","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"function run_simulation!(network,n_spikes,initial_rate)\n  t_now = 0.0\n  H.reset!(network)\n  H.set_initial_rates!(connected_population,initial_rate)\n  for _ in 1:n_spikes\n    t_now = H.dynamics_step!(t_now,network)\n  end\n  return t_now\nend\n\nlast_spike_time = run_simulation!(network,n_spikes,initial_rate)\ntrain = H.get_content(rec_fulltrain)\nbinned_rate = H.get_content(rec_binnedrate)\n\nempirical_rate = train.n_recorded_spikes/last_spike_time\nreached_bins = binned_rate.times .< last_spike_time\n\n(; theoretical_rate,empirical_rate,last_spike_time,\n   n_recorded_spikes=train.n_recorded_spikes,\n   mean_binned_rate=mean(binned_rate.rates[reached_bins]))","title":"Run the simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_single_unit/#Relaxation-toward-the-asymptotic-rate","page":"Single self-interacting unit","text":"The trace starts at initial_rate, while the external input makes the initial conditional intensity h_in + w_self * initial_rate. Its expected value relaxes with time constant τ_kernel/(1-w_self).\n\ninitial_intensity = h_in + w_self*initial_rate\nrelaxation_time = τ_kernel/(1-w_self)\nexpected_rate(t) = theoretical_rate +\n    (initial_intensity-theoretical_rate)*exp(-t/relaxation_time)\n\nplot_end = 8*relaxation_time\nidx_plot = reached_bins .& (binned_rate.times .<= plot_end)\nplot(\n    binned_rate.times[idx_plot],binned_rate.rates[idx_plot];\n    label=\"2 s binned rate\",color=:steelblue,alpha=0.6,\n    xlabel=\"time\",ylabel=\"rate\",ylim=(0,1.8theoretical_rate))\nplot!(\n    t -> expected_rate(t),0,plot_end;\n    label=\"expected relaxation\",color=:black,linewidth=3)\nhline!(\n    [theoretical_rate];\n    label=\"asymptotic rate\",color=:firebrick,linestyle=:dash,linewidth=2)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Relaxation toward the asymptotic rate"},{"category":"section","location":"#Hawkes-Plastic-Networks","page":"Home","text":"(Image: )\n\nwarning: Warning\nHawkesPlasticNetworks.jl is at an early stage. Its API may change as the supported model set grows.\n\nHawkesPlasticNetworks.jl provides explicit building blocks for simulating spiking networks driven by Hawkes processes. Start with the worked example below; the API index lists the currently available components.","title":"Hawkes Plastic Networks"},{"category":"section","location":"#Examples","page":"Home","text":"Single self-interacting unit","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"#Index","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"method","location":"#HawkesPlasticNetworks.apply_plasticity!-Tuple{Real, Symbol, Integer, Tuple}","page":"Home","text":"apply_plasticity!(t_fire, population_fire_label, neuron_fire_idx,\n                  connected_populations)\n\nApply every plasticity rule on every connection after a neuron fires.\n\nRules implement apply_plasticity!(rule, connection, t_fire, population_fire_label, neuron_fire_idx). The tuple recursion keeps the concrete connection and rule types visible to the compiler and does not allocate intermediate collections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HawkesPlasticNetworks.apply_plasticity!"}]
